# Preface

This is the 2026 revision (published in Summer 2026) of my lecture notes for the first quarter of a PhD (or MA) microeconomics course. The lecture notes were developed over a period of more than 30 years during which I taught the material at Tel Aviv University, Princeton University, and New York University.

The book was first published in 2007. At the time, I was hesitant about publishing it since there were several superb books for graduate microeconomics courses already on the shelves. Foremost among them are those of David Kreps. Kreps (1990) covers the material in depth and includes many ideas for future research. Kreps (2013) is even better and is now my favorite for a graduate microeconomics course.

Three other books are on my shortlist: Mas-Colell, Whinston and Green (1995) is very comprehensive; Bowles (2003) returns to the authentic political economics roots of economics; and Jehle and Reny (1997) offers a highly precise style of analysis. All together, these books constitute an impressive collection of textbooks for an advanced microeconomics course.

I am not trying to compete with these books. I am simply offering a somewhat different perspective. In particular, the book never attempts to persuade the reader of how useful economic theory is. The book fits into my view of economic theory as basically a collection of fables (see Rubinstein (2006a, 2012)). The material is presented in a concise manner with emphasis on precision and interpretation rather than generalities and mathematical sophistication.

#### Downloading Updated Versions

As a matter of principle, the book is posted on the web and is available free of charge. I am grateful to Princeton University Press for allowing it to be downloaded for free immediately after publication.

Since 2007, I have updated the book annually, adding material and exercises and of course correcting mistakes. I hope to continue revising the book annually as long as I teach the course. To access the latest electronic version go to: https://arielrubinstein.tau.ac.il or https://arielrubinstein.org

<!-- page 5 -->

#### Solution Manual

The book contains 147 problems (71 within the chapters and 76 review problems). Teachers (and only teachers) of a graduate course in Microeconomics can also get an updated solution manual. Requests for the manual should be made at my homepage or at:

https://arielrubinstein.org/gt/microTheory/

#### Gender

Throughout the book I use only male pronouns. This is my deliberate choice and does not reflect the policy of the editors or the publishers. I believe that continuous reminders of the he/she issue simply divert readers' attention. Language is of course important in shaping our thinking, but I feel it is more effective to raise the issue of discrimination against women in the discussion of "the issues" rather than raising flags on every page of a book on economic theory.

#### Acknowledgments

I would like to thank all of my teaching assistants who made helpful comments during the many years I taught the course:

