Consider the sequence of preference relations  $(\gtrsim^n)_{n=1,2,\dots}$ , defined on  $\mathbb{R}_+^2$  where  $\gtrsim^n$  is represented by the utility function  $u_n(x_1, x_2) = x_1^n + x_2^n$ . We will say that the sequence  $\gtrsim^n$  converges to the preferences  $\gtrsim^*$  if for every  $x$  and  $y$ , such that  $x \succ^* y$ , there is an  $N$  such that for every  $n > N$  we have  $x \succ^n y$ . Show that the sequence of preference relations  $\gtrsim^n$  converges to the preferences  $\gtrsim^*$ , which are represented by the function  $max\{x_1, x_2\}$ .

