# Rationalizing

Economists have often been criticized for making the assumption that decision makers maximize a preference relation. It seems that most decisions are made without explicitly maximizing a preference relation. The most common response to this criticism is that we don't really need to assume that the individual follows the rational man procedure, but only that he can be described *as if* he were maximizing some preference relation.

Accordingly, we will say that a choice function  $C$  *can be rationalized* if there is a preference relation  $\succ$  on  $X$  so that  $C = C_{\succ}$  (i.e.,  $C(A) = C_{\succ}(A)$  for any  $A$  in the domain of  $C$ ).

We will now identify a condition under which a choice function can indeed be presented as if derived from some preference relation (i.e., it can be rationalized).

#### Condition α:

We say that  $C$  satisfies condition  $\alpha$  if for any two problems  $A, B \in D$ , if  $A \subset B$  and  $C(B) \in A$ , then  $C(A) = C(B)$ . (See fig. 3.1.)

Note that if  $\check{x}$  is a preference relation on  $X$ , then  $C_{\check{x}}$  (defined on a set of subsets of  $X$  that have a single most preferred element) satisfies condition  $\alpha$ : if  $C(B) \check{x}$  for all  $x \in B$ , then it is also true that  $C(B) \check{x}$  for all  $x \in A$ .

As an ex example of a choice procedure that does not satisfy condition  $\alpha$ , consider the *second-best choice function*  $C$ : the decision maker has in mind an ordering  $\gtrsim$  of  $X$  (i.e., a complete, asymmetric and transitive binary relation) and  $C(A)$  is the element from  $A$  that is the  $\gtrsim$ -maximal from among the non-maximal alternatives. The choice function  $C$  does

<!-- page 38 -->

not satisfy condition  $\alpha$ : Let  $b$  be the  $\zeta$ -maximal element in  $B$ , and let  $A = B - \{b\}$ . Then,  $C(B) \in A \subset B$  but  $C(A) \neq C(B)$  since  $C(A)$  is the third  $\zeta$ -best in  $B$ .

We will show now that condition  $\alpha$  is a sufficient condition for a choice function to be formulated *as if* the decision maker is maximizing some preference relation.

#### Proposition:

Assume that  $C$  is a choice function defined on a domain containing at least all subsets of  $X$  of size of at most 3. If  $C$  satisfies condition  $\alpha$ , then there is a preference  $\prec$  on  $X$  so that  $C = C_{\prec}$ .

#### Proof:

Define a binary relation  $\lesssim$  by  $x \lesssim y$  if  $x = C(\{x, y\})$ . We first verify that the relation  $\lesssim$  is a preference relation.

We first verify that the relation  $\gtrsim$  is a preference relation.

*Completeness and Reflexivity:* follows from the fact that  $C(\{x, y\})$  is always well defined.

*Transitivity:* If  $x \asymp y$  and  $y \asymp z$ , then  $C(\{x, y\}) = x$  and  $C(\{y, z\}) = y$ . By condition  $\alpha$  and  $C(\{x, y\}) = x$ ,  $C(\{x, y, z\}) \neq y$ . By condition  $\alpha$  and  $C(\{y, z\}) = y$ ,  $C(\{x, y, z\}) \neq z$ . Therefore,  $C(\{x, y, z\}) = x$  and by condition  $\alpha$  we must have  $C(\{x, z\}) = x$ , that is,  $x \asymp z$ .

We still need to show that  $C(B) = C_{\sim}(B)$  for all  $B$ . Let  $C(B) = x$  and let  $y \in B - \{x\}$ . Condition  $\alpha$  implies that  $C(\{x, y\}) = x$ . Thus,  $x \lesssim y$  for all  $y \in B$ .

Following is a different version of the above proposition:

#### Proposition:

Let  $C$  be a choice function with a domain  $D$  satisfying that if  $A, B \in D$ , then  $A \cup B \in D$ . If  $C$  satisfies condition  $\alpha$ , then there is a preference relation  $\succ$  on  $X$  such that  $C = C_{\succ}$ .

#### Proof:

Define a binary relation as  $xRy$  if  $x$  and  $y$  are distinct and there is a set  $A \in D$  such that  $y \in A$  and  $c(A) = x$ . Note that  $R$  is not necessarily complete. We will see that the relation  $R$  does not have cycles.

But first note the following: If  $xRy$  and  $yRz$ , then there is  $A \in D$  containing  $y$  such that  $C(A) = x$  and there is  $B \in D$  containing  $z$  such that  $C(B) = y$ . The set  $A \cup B$  is a member of  $D$ . The element  $C(A \cup B)$ 

<!-- page 39 -->

is in either  $A$  or  $B$  and thus it is either  $x$  or  $y$  by condition  $\alpha$ . It is not  $y$  since if  $C(A \cup B) = y \in A$  then by condition  $\alpha$ ,  $C(A \cup B) = C(A) = y$ . Thus,  $C(A \cup B) = x$ . Condition  $\alpha$  and  $C(B) = y$  imply that  $x \notin B$  and thus  $z \neq x$ . Therefore,  $xRz$ .

Assume now that a cycle of  $R$  exists and thus there is a shortest cycle. By the above, it cannot be of length 2 and if it is of length 3 or more it can be shortened. Therefore,  $R$  does not have a cycle.

A well-known proposition in Set Theory (which you proved for finite sets in Problem 4 of Problem Set 1) guarantees that the acyclic and anti-symmetric relation  $R$  extends to a preference relation  $\succ$ . By definition,  $c(A) \succ x$  for all  $x \in A$  and therefore  $C(A) = C \succ (A)$ .

## The Satisficing Procedure

The fact that if  $D$  is sufficiently broad we can present any choice function satisfying condition  $\alpha$  as an outcome of the optimization of some preference relation supports the view that the scope of microeconomic models goes beyond situations in which agents simply carry out explicit optimization. But have we indeed expanded the scope of these models significantly? Are there reasonable choice functions satisfying condition  $\alpha$  which are naturally described without reference to maximization of some preference relation?

Consider the following “choice scheme”, which was given the name *satisficing* by Herbert Simon. Let  $v : X \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$  be a valuation of the elements in  $X$ , and let  $v^* \in \mathbb{R}$  be a threshold of satisfaction. Let  $O$  be an ordering of the alternatives in  $X$ . Given a set  $A$ , the decision maker arranges the elements of this set in a list  $L(A, O)$  according to the ordering  $O$ . He then chooses the first element in  $L(A, O)$  that has a  $v$ -value at least as large as  $v^*$ . If there is no such element in  $A$ , the decision maker chooses the last element in  $L(A, O)$ .

Any choice function which belongs to this scheme is characterized by its domain, the function  $v$ , the threshold  $v^*$  and the ordering  $O$ . We now show that any such function satisfies condition  $\alpha$ . Assume that  $a$  is chosen from  $B$  and is also a member of  $A \subset B$ . The list  $L(A, O)$  is obtained from  $L(B, O)$  by eliminating all elements in  $B - A$ . If  $v(a) \geq v^*$ , then  $a$  is the first satisfactory element in both  $L(B, O)$  and  $L(A, O)$ . Thus,  $a$  is chosen from  $A$ . If all elements in  $B$  are unsatisfactory, then  $a$  must be the last element in  $L(B, O)$ . Since  $A$  is a subset of  $B$ , all elements in  $A$  are unsatisfactory and  $a$  is the last element in  $L(A, O)$ . Thus,  $a$  is chosen from  $A$ .

<!-- page 40 -->

Without making any assumptions about the domain of such a choice function, we now show that it can be rationalized by explicitly constructing an ordering that rationalizes it. Let  $\asymp$  be the ordering that places the elements that satisfice (namely, the members of  $\{x | v(x) \geq v^*\}$ ) on top, ordered according to  $O$ . The other alternatives are placed at the bottom, ordered according to the reversed  $O$ . For any set  $A$ , maximizing  $\asymp$  will yield the first element (according to  $O$ ) which is satisficing and if there aren't any then maximization leads to the choice of the last element in  $A$  (according to  $O$ ).

Note, however, that even after a “small” modification the procedure no longer satisfies condition  $\alpha$  and thus cannot be rationalized. For example:

*Satisfying using two orderings:* Let  $X$  be a population of candidates for a job. If there are less than 5, order them alphabetically. If there are at least 5, order them by their social security number. Whatever ordering is used, choose the first candidate whose average grade in high-school is above 85 and if there are none, choose the last student on the list. Condition  $\alpha$  might not be satisfied in this case. It may be that  $a$  is the first candidate with a satisfactory grade in a long list of students ordered by social security number. However,  $a$  might not be the first candidate with a satisfactory grade on a list of only three of the candidates who appeared on the list when ordered alphabetically.

To summarize, the satisficing procedure, though stated in a way that seems unrelated to the maximization of a preference relation or utility function, can be viewed *as if* the decision maker is maximizing a preference relation. I know of no other interesting general choice scheme that satisfies condition  $\alpha$  other than the “rational man” and the satisficing procedures. However, in the discussion of the classical consumer model, we will come across several appealing examples of demand functions that can be rationalized, even though they appear to be unrelated to the maximization of a preference relation.

# What is an Alternative?

Some of the cases in which rationality is violated can be attributed to the incorrect specification of the space of alternatives. Consider the following example taken from Luce and Raiffa (1957): A diner in a restaurant will choose *chicken* from the menu of *steak tartare*, *chicken* but will choose *steak tartare* from the menu of *steak tartare*, *chicken*, *frog legs*. At first glance, the behavior appears not to be rational (since his choice violates

<!-- page 41 -->

condition  $\alpha$ ). His choice does make sense if he believes that the presence of *frog legs* on the menu is a positive indication of the quality of the chef. Thus, if the dish *frog legs* is on the menu, the cook must be a real expert, and the decision maker is happy to order *steak tartare*, which requires expertise to make. If the menu lacks *frog legs*, the decision maker does not want to take the risk of choosing *steak tartare*.

Rationality is “restored” if we make the distinction between “*steak tartare* served in a restaurant where *frog legs* are also on the menu (and the cook must then be a real chef)” and “*steak tartare* in a restaurant where *frog legs* are not served (and the cook is likely to be a novice)”. Such a distinction makes sense because *steak tartare* is not the same in the two choice sets.

Note that if we define an alternative to be  $(a, A)$ , where  $a$  is a physical description and  $A$  is the choice problem from which it is chosen, any choice function  $C$  can be trivially rationalized by a preference relation satisfying  $(C(A), A) \succ (a, A)$  for every  $a \in A$ .

The lesson from the above discussion is that care must be taken in specifying the term “alternative”. An alternative  $a$  must have the same meaning in every choice problem  $A$  that contains  $a$ .

# Choice Correspondences

The choice function definition we have been using so far requires that a single element be assigned to each choice problem. If the decision maker follows the rational man procedure using a preference relation with indifferences, the previously defined induced choice function  $C_{\sim}(A)$  might be undefined because for some choice problems there would be more than one maximal element. This is one of the reasons that we sometimes use the alternative framework of choice correspondence.

A *choice correspondence*  $C$  is required to assign to every nonempty  $A \in D$  a nonempty *subset* of  $A$ , that is,  $\emptyset \neq C(A) \subseteq A$ . According to our interpretation of a choice problem, a decision maker selects a unique element from every choice set. Thus,  $C(A)$  cannot be interpreted as the choice made by the decision maker when he chooses from  $A$ . The revised interpretation of  $C(A)$  is the *set* of all elements in  $A$  that are satisfactory, in the sense that if the decision maker is about to make a decision and chooses  $a \in C(A)$ , then he has no desire to move away from it. In other words, the induced choice correspondence reflects an “internal equilibrium”: if the decision maker facing  $A$  considers an alternative outside of  $C(A)$ , then he will continue searching for another alternative.

<!-- page 42 -->

![](pg_21__page_22_Figure_1.jpeg)

> **Image description:** Venn diagram showing two overlapping large circular sets, labeled A on the left and B on the right. Inside set A is an oval region labeled C(A) with a dotted pattern, which extends into the intersection of A and B. Inside set B is another oval region labeled C(B) with a grid/hatched pattern, which also overlaps into the intersection of A and B and intersects with C(A). Point x is located within C(A) inside the intersection of sets A and B, but outside C(B). Point y is located inside both C(A) and C(B) within the intersection of sets A and B. On the top right, three mathematical relations are listed: x in C(A), y in C(B), and x not in C(B).

**Figure 3.2**  
Violation of the weak axiom.

If he happens to consider an alternative inside  $C(A)$ , then that is the one he will choose.

A related interpretation of  $C(A)$  views it as the set of all elements in  $A$  that might be chosen under any of the many possible circumstances not included in the description of the set  $A$ . Formally, let  $(A, f)$  be an extended choice set where  $f$  is the frame that accompanies the set  $A$  (such as the default alternative or the order of the alternatives). Let  $c(A, f)$  be the choice of the decision maker from the choice set  $A$  given the frame  $f$ . The (extended) choice *function*  $c$  induces the choice *correspondence*  $C(A) = \{x | x = c(A, f) \text{ for some } f\}$ .

Given a preference relation  $\prec$ , we define the induced choice correspondence (assuming it is never empty) as  $C_{\prec}(A) = \{x \in A \mid x \prec y \text{ for all } y \in A\}$ .

#### The Weak Axiom of Revealed Preference (WA):

When  $x, y \in A$  and  $x \in C(A)$ , we say that  $x$  is revealed to be at least as good as  $y$ . If, in addition,  $y \notin C(A)$ , we say that  $x$  is revealed to be strictly better than  $y$ . Condition  $\alpha$  is now replaced by condition WA, which requires that if  $x$  is revealed to be at least as good as  $y$ , then  $y$  is not revealed to be strictly better than  $x$ .

We say that  $C$  satisfies WA if whenever  $x, y \in A \cap B$ ,  $x \in C(A)$ , and  $y \in C(B)$ , it is also true that  $x \in C(B)$  (fig. 3.2).

Notice that if  $C(A)$  contains all the elements that are maximal according to some preference relation, then  $C$  satisfies WA.

<!-- page 43 -->

The Weak Axiom trivially implies two properties of choice correspondences that are often discussed in the literature:

*Condition  $\alpha$ :* If  $a \in A \subset B$  and  $a \in C(B)$ , then  $a \in C(A)$ .  
*Condition  $\beta$ :* If  $a, b \in A \subset B$ ,  $a, b \in C(A)$  and  $b \in C(B)$ , then  $a \in C(A)$ .

*Condition  $\beta$ :* If  $a, b \in A \subset B$ ,  $a, b \in C(A)$ , and  $b \in C(B)$ , then  $a \in C(B)$ . Verify that conditions  $\alpha$  and  $\beta$  together are equivalent to WA for any

Verify that conditions  $\alpha$  and  $\beta$  together are equivalent to WA for any choice correspondence with a domain satisfying that if  $A$  and  $B$  are included in the domain, then so is their intersection.

The two properties are independent as is demonstrated by the following two examples of choice correspondences (with domains including all subsets of  $X$ ):

- 1. The decision maker has in mind a strict preference relation and  $C(A)$  contains the top two elements in  $A$ . The correspondence satisfies condition  $\alpha$  but not  $\beta$ .
- 2. The decision maker has in mind a complete directed graph  $\rightarrow$ . If the set  $A$  contains an element  $a$  such that  $a \rightarrow x$  for all  $x \in A - \{a\}$ , then  $C(A) = \{a\}$ . Otherwise,  $C(A) = A$ . Unless  $\rightarrow$  is transitive, the correspondence satisfies condition  $\beta$  but not  $\alpha$ .

For the next proposition, we could have made do with a weaker version of WA, which includes the same condition but applied only to any twone sets  $A \subset B$  where  $A$  is a set of two elements.

#### Proposition:

Assume that  $C$  is a choice correspondence with a domain that includes at least all subsets of size at most 3. Assume that  $C$  satisfies properties  $\alpha$  and  $\beta$ . Then, there is a preference  $\succ$  such that  $C = C_{\succ}$ .

#### Proof:

Define  $x \asymp y$  if  $x \in C(\{x, y\})$ . We will now show that this relation is a preference:

*Reflexivity:* follows from  $C(\{x\}) = x$ .  
*Completeness:* follows from  $C(\{x \in \mathbb{N}\})$ 

*Completeness:* follows from  $C(\{x, y\}) \neq \emptyset$ .  
*Transitivity:* If  $x \succeq y$  and  $y \succeq z$  then  $x \in C$ .

*Transitivity:* If  $x \gtrsim y$  and  $y \gtrsim z$ , then  $x \in C(\{x, y\})$  and  $y \in C(\{y, z\})$ . If  $y \in C(\{x, y, z\})$  then by condition  $\alpha$  we get  $y \in C(\{x, y\})$  and condition  $\beta$  implies that  $x \in C(\{x, y, z\})$ . Similarly, if  $z \in C(\{x, y, z\})$ , then  $y \in C(\{x, y, z\})$ . Thus, in all cases,  $x \in C(\{x, y, z\})$ . By condition  $\alpha$ ,  $x \in C(\{x, z\})$  and therefore  $x \gtrsim z$ .

It remains to be shown that  $C(B) = C_{\mathfrak{L}}(B)$ . As in the case  $C(E)$ ,  $E = \mathbb{R}^n$  with  $n = 2$ .

Assume that  $x \in C(B)$ . By condition  $\alpha$  for every  $y \in B$  we have  $x \in C(\{x, y\})$  and thus  $x \subsetneq y$ . It follows that  $x \in C_{\subsetneq}(B)$ .

<!-- page 44 -->

Assume that  $x \in C_{\infty}(B)$  and let  $y \in C(B)$ . If  $y \neq x$ , then  $x \in C(\{x, y\})$  and by condition  $\alpha$  we have  $y \in C(\{x, y\})$ . Using condition  $\beta$  we conclude that  $x \in C(B)$ .

# Psychological Motives Not Included within the Framework

A sharp critique on the standard approach to modeling economic agents came from psychologists, most notably Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman. They provided numerous examples which demonstrate not only that rationality is often violated (this is very easy) but that there are systematic reasons for those violations which can be attributed to specific elements of our decision procedures. Following are a few examples:

#### Framing

The following experiment (see Tversky and Kahneman (1986)) illustrates that the way in which alternatives are framed may affect decision makers' choices. Subjects were asked to imagine being confronted by the following dramatic choice problem:

An outbreak of disease is expected to cause 600 deaths in the United States. Two mutually exclusive programs are expected to yield the following results:

- (a) 400 people will die.
- (b) With probability 1/3, 0 people will die, and with probability 2/3, 600 people will die.

A second group of subjects was given the same background information and asked to choose from the following alternatives:

- (c) 200 people will be saved.
- (d) With probability 1/3, all 600 will be saved, and with probability 2/3, none will be saved.

Whereas 78% of the first group chose (b), only 28% of the second group chose (d). These are "problematic" results since all agree that (a) and (c) are identical alternatives, as are (b) and (d). Thus, the first choice should be consistent with the second choice.

Using my didactic experimental website, both versions were presented in the above order to about 10,000 students in game theory courses with

<!-- page 45 -->

the result that 72% chose (b) and 49% chose (d). It seems plausible that many of them recalled their answer to the first question when responding to the second one, and therefore the level of inconsistency was reduced. Nonetheless, a large proportion of students gave different answers to the two problems, presented one after the other (probably with some pose between), which makes the findings even more striking.

The results demonstrate the sensitivity of choice to the framing of the alternatives. What is more basic to rational decision making than taking the same choice when only the manner in which the problems are stated is different?

## Simplifying the Choice Problem and the Use of Similarities

The following experiment was also conducted by Tversky and Kahneman. One group of subjects was presented with the following choice problem:

Choose one of the two roulette games *a* or *b*. Your prize is the one corresponding to the outcome of the roulette game you chose as specified in the following tables:

| ( a ) | Color    | White | Red | Green | Yellow |
|-------|----------|-------|-----|-------|--------|
|       | Chance % | 90    | 6   | 1     | 3      |
|       | Prize \$ | 0     | 45  | 30    | − 15   |
| ( b ) |          |       |     |       |        |
|       | Color    | White | Red | Green | Yellow |
|       | Chance % | 90    | 7   | 1     | 2      |
|       | Prize \$ | 0     | 45  | − 10  | − 15   |

A different group of subjects was presented with the same background information and had the choice between the following options:

| ( c ) | Color    | White | Red | Green | Blue | Yellow |
|-------|----------|-------|-----|-------|------|--------|
|       | Chance % | 90    | 6   | 1     | 1    | 2      |
|       | Prize \$ | 0     | 45  | 30    | − 15 | − 15   |
| ( d ) |          |       |     |       |      |        |
|       | Color    | White | Red | Green | Blue | Yellow |
|       | Chance % | 90    | 6   | 1     | 1    | 2      |
|       | Prize \$ | 0     | 45  | 45    | − 10 | − 15   |

In the original experiment, 58% of the subjects in the first group chose  $a$ , whereas no one in the second group chose  $c$ . When the two problems were presented on my website one after the other, 51% of about 5,000

<!-- page 46 -->

students chose *a* and 7% chose *c*. Interestingly, the median response time among the students who answered *a* was 52 seconds, whereas among the students who answered *b* it was much higher – 87 seconds.

The results demonstrate a procedure commonly used by people when confronted with a complicated choice problem. We are often transforming a complicated problem into a simpler one by “canceling” similar elements. Although  $d$  clearly dominates  $c$ , the comparison between  $a$  and  $b$  is not as straightforward. Many subjects “cancel” out the probabilities of White, Yellow, and Red and are left with comparing only the Green prizes, a process that leads to the choice of  $a$ .

Incidentally, when I have presented these choice problems in class, there were students (some of the best ones, in fact) t) who chose  $c$ . According to their explanations, they treated the second problem as identical to the first and followed the following reasoning: “I chose  $a$  from  $\{a, b\}$ . The alternatives  $c$  and  $d$  are identical to the alternatives  $a$  and  $b$ , respectively. It is only natural then that I choose  $c$  from  $\{c, d\}$ .” This observation illustrates that our model of choice does not allow for dependence of choice on previous choices made by the decision maker.

### Reason-Based Choice

Making choices sometimes involves finding reasons to pick one alternative over another. When the deliberation involves reasons strongly associated with the problem at hand (“internal reasons”), we often find it difficult to reconcile the choice with the rational man paradigm.

For example, imagine a European student applying to graduate schools who would choose *Princeton* from  $\{\text{Princeton}, \text{LSE}\}$  and *LSE* from  $\{\text{Princeton}, \text{Chicago}, \text{LSE}\}$ . According to his explanation, he prefers an American university as long as he does not have to make the hard choice between two of them. When choosing from  $\{\text{Princeton}, \text{Chicago}, \text{LSE}\}$ , he finds it difficult to decide between *Princeton* and *Chicago* and therefore chooses not to cross the Atlantic. His choice does not satisfy condition  $\alpha$ , not because of a careless specification of the alternatives (as in the restaurant menu example discussed previously), but because his reasoning involves an attempt to avoid a more difficult decision.

A better example was suggested to me by a former NYU student Federico Filippini: “Imagine there’s a handsome guy called Albert who is looking for a date to take to a party. Albert knows two girls,named Mary and Laura, who are crazy about him and would love to go to the party. Of the two, Albert prefers Mary. Now imagine that Mary has a sister, who is also crazy about Albert. Albert must now choose between

<!-- page 47 -->

Mary, Mary's sister, and Laura. I bet that if Albert is rational, he will be taking Laura to the party."

Another example follows Huber, Payne, and Puto (1982):

Let  $x = (x_1, x_2)$  be “a holiday package of  $x_1$  days in Paris and  $x_2$  days in London”. Choose one of the four vectors  $a = (7, 4)$ ,  $b = (4, 7)$ ,  $c = (6, 3)$ , and  $d = (3, 6)$ .

All subjects in the experiment agreed that a day in Paris and a day in London are desirable goods. Some of the subjects were asked to choose between the three alternatives *a*, *b*, and *c* while others were asked to choose between *a*, *b*, and *d*. The subjects exhibited a tendency toward choosing *a* from  $\{a, b, c\}$  and *b* from  $\{a, b, d\}$ . A related experiment is reported in Shafir, Simonson, and Tversky (1993). A group of subjects was asked to imagine choosing between a camera costing \$170 and a superior camera, produced by the same company, costing \$240. Another group was asked to imagine they have to choose between three cameras – the two described above and a third, much more sophisticated camera costing \$470. The addition of the third alternative significantly increased the proportion of subjects who chose the \$240 camera. The commonsense explanation is that subjects faced a conflict between two desires: to buy a better camera and to pay less. They resolved the conflict by choosing the “compromise alternative”.

To conclude, decision makers often look for reasons to prefer one alternative over another. Typically, making decisions based on “external reasons” (which do not relate to the properties of the choice set) will not lead to violations of rationality. On the other hand, applying “internal reasons”, such as “I prefer the alternative *a* over the alternative *b* because *a* clearly dominates the other alternative *c* while *b* does not” might result in violations of condition  $\alpha$ .

#### Mental Accounting

The following example is taken from Kahneman and Tversky (1984). A group of subjects were presented with the following question:

- 1. Imagine that you have decided to see a play and paid \$20 for a ticket. As you enter the theater, you discover that you have lost the ticket. The seat was not marked and the ticket cannot be reissued. Would you pay \$20 for another?

Members of another group were asked the following question:

- 2. Imagine that you have decided to see a play where the tickets cost \$20. As you arrive at the theater, you discover that you have lost a \$20 bill. Would you still pay \$20 for a ticket to the play?

<!-- page 48 -->

If the rational man cares only about seeing the play and his wealth, he should realize that there is no difference between the consequence of replying Yes to question 1 and Yes to question 2 (in both cases he will own a ticket and will be poorer by \$40). Similarly, there is no difference between the consequence of replying No to question 1 and No to question 2. Thus, the rational agent should give the same answer to both questions. Nonetheless, only 46% said they would buy another ticket after they had lost the first one, whereas 88% said they would buy a ticket after losing the \$20 bill. (In the data I collected from 2,600 participants, the gap was much smaller: 66% vs. 79%.) It is likely that participants made a calculation in which they compared the “mental price” of a ticket to its subjective value. Many of those who decided not to buy another ticket after losing the first attributed a price of \$40 to the ticket and attributed the price of \$20 to the ticket after they lost the \$20 bill. Thus, these kind of “mental calculations” may be inconsistent with rationality.

# Modeling Choice Procedures

There is a large body of evidence that decision makers systematically use procedures of choice that violate the classical assumptions and that the rational man paradigm is lacking. As a result economists have introduced models in which economic agents are assumed to use alternative procedures of choice. This section describes one line of research that attempts to incorporate such decision makers into economic models.

We wish to enrich the concept of a choice problem to include not only the set of alternatives but also a *default option*. The statement  $c(A, a) = b$  means that when facing the choice problem  $A$  when the default alternative is  $a$  the decision maker chooses  $b$ . Experimental evidence and introspection tell us that being a default boosts the alternative, a phenomenon known as *status quo bias*.

Let  $X$  be a finite set of alternatives. Define an *extended choice function* to be a function that assigns a unique element in  $A$  to every pair  $(A, a)$  where  $A \subseteq X$  and  $a \in A$ .

<!-- page 49 -->

Following are some examples of extended choice functions that demonstrate the richness of the concept. In some of the examples the decision maker has in mind an ordering  $\succ$  interpreted as the real decision maker's preference relation.

- 1. The decision maker also has in mind a partial ordering  $D$  where  $aDb$  is interpreted as " $a$  clearly dominates  $b$ " (probably because all considerations favor  $b$  over  $a$ ) The alternative  $C(A, a)$  is the  $\Sigma$ -best element in  $A$  that dominate  $a$ , as well as the default alternative (i.e.,  $\{x \mid xDa\} \cup \{a\}$ ).
  2. Let  $d$  be a distance function on  $X$ . The element  $C(A, a)$  is the
- Let  $d$  be a distance function on  $X$ . The element  $C(A, a)$  is the  $\Sigma$ -best alternative that is not too far from  $a$  (i.e. it lies within  $\{x \mid d(x, a) \leq d^*\}$  for some  $d^*$ ).
  The decision maker has in mind  $\geq$  an ordering of  $X$ . The element
- 3. The decision maker has in mind  $\supseteq$ , an ordering of  $X$ . The element  $C(A, a)$  is the first alternative in  $A$  which appears in the ordering just below  $a$  and  $a$  if  $a$  is the  $\supseteq$  - minimal element in  $A$ .
- 4. Buridan's donkey: The decision maker has a preference relation in mind. If there is a unique alternative which is  $\gtrsim$ -better than the default, then it is chosen. If not, then the decision maker stays with the default option (since he cannot make up his mind) (see the item Buridan's ass in Wikipedia).
- 5. Just not the status quo: The decision maker has in mind preferences  $\succ$  on  $X$ . Given  $(A, a)$ , he chooses the  $\succ$ -best alternative in  $A - \{a\}$ .

In the rest of the section, we will characterize the set of extended choice functions that are induced by the following procedure. The decision maker is characterized by a “utility function”  $u$  and a “bias function”  $\beta$ , which assigns a non-negative number to each alternative. The function  $u$  is interpreted as representing the “true” preferences. The number  $\beta(x)$  is interpreted as the bonus attached to  $x$  when it is the default alternative. Given an extended choice problem  $(A, a)$ , the choice procedure, denoted by  $DBP_{u,\beta}$ , is as follows:

$$DBP_{u,\beta}(A, a) = \begin{cases} x \in A - \{a\} & \text{if } u(x) > u(a) + \beta(a) \text{ and } u(x) > u(y) \\ & \text{for any } y \in A - \{a, x\} \\ a & \text{if } u(a) + \beta(a) \geq u(x), \forall x \in A - \{a\} \end{cases}$$

<!-- page 50 -->

We will adopt two assumptions:

#### The Weak Axiom (WA)

We say that an extended choice function  $c$  satisfies the Weak Axiom if there are no sets  $A$  and  $B$ ,  $a, b \in A \cap B$ ,  $a \neq b$  and  $x, y \notin \{a, b\}$  ( $x$  and  $y$  are not necessarily distinct) such that:

- 1.  $c(A, a) = a$  and  $c(B, a) = b$  or
- 2.  $c(A, x) = a$  and  $c(B, y) = b$ .

The Weak Axiom states that:

- 1. If  $a$  is revealed to be better than  $b$  in a choice problem where  $a$  is the default, then there cannot be any choice problem in which  $b$  is revealed to be better than  $a$  when  $a$  is the default.
- 2. If  $a$  is revealed to be better than  $b$  in a choice problem where neither  $a$  nor  $b$  is a default, then there cannot be any choice problem in which  $b$  is revealed to be better than  $a$  when again neither  $a$  nor  $b$  is the default.

## Comment:

The setet of all subsets of  $X$  that contain  $a$  satisfies the union property and thus WA (which implies property  $\alpha$ ) implies that for every  $a \in X$  there is a preference relation  $\succ_a$  such that  $c(A, a)$  is the  $\succ_a$ -maximal in  $A$ . However,  $\succ_a$  might be different from  $\succ_b$ .

#### Default Tendency (DT)

We say that an extended choice function  $c$  satisfies Default Tendency if for every set  $A$ ,  $c(A, x) = a$  implies that  $c(A, a) = a$ .

This assumption states that if the decision maker chooses  $a$  from a set  $A$  when  $x \neq a$  is the default, he does not change his mind if  $x$  is replaced by  $a$  as the default alternative.

#### Proposition:

An extended choice function  $c$  satisfies WA and DT if and only if it is a default-bias procedure.

<!-- page 51 -->

#### Proof:

Consider a default-bias procedure  $c$  characterized by the functions  $u$  and  $\beta$ . It satisfies:

- DT: if  $c(A, x) = a$  and  $x \neq a$ , then  $u(a) > u(y)$  for any  $y \neq a$  in  $A$ . Thus, also  $u(a) + \beta(a) > u(y)$  for any  $y \neq a$  in  $A$  and  $c(A, a) = a$ .
  WA: for any two sets  $A, B, a, b \in A \cap B$ ,  $a \neq b$ .
- WA: for any two sets  $A, B$ ,  $a, b \in A \cap B$ ,  $a \neq b$ :

In the other direction, let  $c$  be an extended choice function satisfying WA and DT. Define a relation  $\succ$  on  $X \times \{0, 1\}$  as follows:

- For any pair  $(A, x)$  for which  $c(A, x) = x$  and for any  $y \in A - \{x\}$ , define  $(x, 1) \succ (y, 0)$ .
  For any pair  $(A, x)$  for which  $c(A, x) = y \neq x$  and for any  $z \in A -$
- For any pair  $(A, x)$  for which  $c(A, x) = y \neq x$  and for any  $z \in A - \{x, y\}$ , define  $(y, 0) \succ (x, 1)$  and  $(y, 0) \succ (z, 0)$ .
  For all  $x \in X$  ( $x \neq 1$ )  $\succ (x, 0)$ .
- For all  $x \in X$ ,  $(x, 1) \succ (x, 0)$ .

The relation is n not complete and might not be transitive, but by WA it is asymmetric. We will see that  $\succ$  can be extended to a full ordering over  $X \times \{0, 1\}$  denoted by  $\succ^*$ . Using problem 4 in Problem Set 1, we only need to show that the relation does not have cycles.

First note that:

- a. For no  $x$  and and  $y$  is  $(x, 0) \succ (y, 0) \succ (x, 1)$  since otherwise there is a set  $A$  containing  $x$  and  $y$  and another alternative  $z \in A$  such that  $c(A, z) = x$ . By DT, also  $c(A, x) = x$  and thus  $(x, 1) \succ (y, 0)$ , contradicting WA.

Assume that  $\succ$  has a cycle and consider a shortest cycle. By WA, there is no cycle of length two, and therefore a shortest cycle has to be of at least length three.

Steps (b) and (c) establish that it is impossible for the shortest cycle to contain a consecutive pair  $(x, 0) \succ (y, 0)$ .

- b. Assume that the cycle contains a consecutive segment  $(x, 0) \succ (y, 0) \succ (z, 1)$ . There is a set  $A$  such that  $c(A, z) = y$ . By (a),  $z \neq x$ . By WA,  $c(A \cup (x \}, z) \in \{x, y\}$  and since  $(x, 0) \succ (y, 0)$  we have  $c(A \cup (x \}, z) = x$  and  $(x, 0) \succ (z, 1)$ . Therefore, we can shorten the cycle.

<!-- page 52 -->

- c. Assume that the cycle contains a consecutive segment of the type  $(x, 0) \succ (y, 0) \succ (z, 0)$ . By WA,  $z \neq x$ . Since  $(y, 0) \succ (z, 0)$ , there exists a set  $A$  containing  $y$  and  $z$  and a different  $a \in A$  such that  $c(A, a) = y$ . By (a),  $a \neq x$  and therefore  $c(A \cup \{x\}, a) = x$  and  $(x, 0) \succ (z, 0)$ , allowing us to shorten the cycle.

Then next two steps establish that it is impossible for the shortest cycle to contain a consecutive pair  $(x, 0) \succ (y, 1)$ .

- d. Assume that the cycle contains a consecutive segment of the type  $(x, 0) \succ (y, 1) \succ (z, 0)$  and  $y \neq z$ . By WA,  $z \neq x$ . Therefore, we get  $c(\{x, y, z\}, y) = x$  and  $(x, 0) \succ (z, 0)$ , thus allowing us to shorten the cycle.
- e. Assume that the cycle contains a consecutive segment of the type  $(x, 0) \succ (y, 1) \succ (y, 0) \succ (z, 1)$ . By DT,  $z \neq x$  and by definition  $z \neq y$ . What is  $c\{\{x, y, z\}, z\}$ ? By WA and  $(y, 0) \succ (z, 1)$ , it is not  $z$ . If it is  $x$ , then  $(x, 0) \succ (y, 0)$  and we can shorten the cycle. If it

Finally, let  $v$  be a utility function representing  $\succ^*$ . Define  $u(x) = v(x, 0)$  and  $\beta(x) = v(x, 1) - v(x, 0)$  to obtain the result.

- 1. If  $c(A, a) = a$ , then  $(a, 1) \succ (x, 0)$  for all  $x \in A - \{a\}$  and thus  $u(a) \succ \beta(a) > u(x)$  for all  $x$ , that is,  $c(A, a) = DBP_{u, \beta}(A, a)$ .
- 2. If  $c(A, a) = x$ , then  $(x, 0) \succ (a, 1)$  and  $(x, 0) \succ (y, 0)$  for all  $y \in A - \{a, x\}$  and therefore  $u(x) > u(a) + \beta(a)$  and  $u(x) > u(y)$  for all  $y \in A - \{a, x\}$ . Thus,  $c(A, a) = DBP_{u, \beta}(A, a)$ .

#### A Comment on the Significance of Axiomatization

There is something aesthetically attractive about axiomatizing choice procedures. However, I disagree with the approach that such an axiomatization is necessary in order to develop a model in which the procedure appears. Too often the axioms make sense only when we have in mind the choice scheme we want to axiomatize. I take the view that a necessary condition for an axiomatization of a choice scheme to be of interest is the possibility of coming up with examples of sensible procedures of choice that satisfy the axioms and are not specified explicitly in the language of the procedure we are axiomatizing. Can one find such a procedure for the above axiomatization? I myself am unable to. Indeed, many of the axiomatizations in this field lack such examples, and therefore, in spite of their aesthetic value (and although I have done some such axiomatizations myself), I find them to be futile exercises.

<!-- page 53 -->

# Bibliographic Notes

An excellent book on Choice Theory is Kreps (1988). On consistency in choice and revealed preference assumptions, see Samuelson (1948), Houthakker (1950), and Richter (1966). Simon (1955) is the source for the discussion of satisficing. For a discussion of the bounded rationality approach to choice, see Rubinstein (1998). Sen (1993) provides a more philosophical discussion of choice. An excellent introduction to the Dutch Book arguments is Yaari (1985). Kahneman and Tversky (2000) is the definitive textbook on the psychological critique of the economic approach to rationality. Rabin (1998) surveys the modern Economics and Psychology approach. The DBP procedure was axiomatized by Masatliglu and Ok (2005); the simpler axiomatization presented here is due to Rubinstein and Salant (2006b).

