# Demand: Consumer Choice

# The consumer's choice function

In this lecture we continue to have  $X = \mathbb{R}_+^K$ , the set of bundles of commodities indexed  $1, 2, \dots, K$ . A choice problem is now a subset of those bundles. Since we are laying the foundation for “price models”, we are interested in the consumer’s choice in a particular class of choice problems called budget sets. A *budget set* is a set of bundles that can be represented as  $B(p, w) = \{x \in X \mid p \cdot x \leq w\}$ , where  $p$  is a vector of positive numbers (interpreted as prices in terms of the wealth unit) and  $w$  is a positive number (interpreted as the consumer’s wealth).

Obviously, any set  $B(p, w)$  is closed since it is defined by  $K + 1$  weak inequalities ( $0 \leq x_k$  for all  $k$  and  $p \cdot x \leq w$ ) and bounded (since for any  $x \in B(p, w)$  and for all  $k$ ,  $0 \leq x_k \leq w/p_k$ ). Thus, it is compact.

Any set  $B(p, w)$  is also convex: Let  $x, y \in B(p, w)$  and  $\alpha \in [0, 1]$ . To see that  $\alpha x + (1 - \alpha)y \in B(p, w)$  note that:

$$p \cdot [\alpha x + (1 - \alpha)y] = \alpha p \cdot x + (1 - \alpha)p \cdot y \leq w$$
 and  $[\alpha x + (1 - \alpha)y]_k = \alpha x_k + (1 - \alpha)y_k \geq 0$  for all  $k$ . A choice function in this context is a function  $x$ 

A choice function in this context is a function  $x(p, w)$  that assigns to every vector of positive numbers (of length  $K + 1$ ) a unique bundle in  $B(p, w)$ . Since we assume that the consumer's choice depends only on the budget set, independently of the framing of the set, and since  $B(\lambda p, \lambda w) = B(p, w)$  we restrict  $x$  to satisfy  $x(p, w) = x(\lambda p, \lambda w)$  (i.e. the function is *homogeneous of degree zero*). We refer to the function  $x(p, w)$  as a *demand function*. Sometimes we will allow  $x(p, w)$  to be a choice correspondence (which attaches to a budget set a nonempty set of bundles) and refer to it as *demand correspondence*. The equality  $x(p, w) = x(\lambda p, \lambda w)$  should not be interpreted as implying that “uniform inflation does not matter.” We have *assumed*, rather than concluded, that the consumer chooses from a set independently of how the choice set is framed. Furthermore, our model of choice is static and it is assumed that the choice is not affected by previous choices. We need to relax at least one of those assumptions in order to model inflation.

<!-- page 77 -->

# The consumer's problem

We will refer to the problem of finding the  $\zeta$ -best bundle in  $B(p, w)$  as the *consumer problem*.

#### Claim:

If  $\check{\times}$  is a continuous relation, then any consumer problem has a solution.

#### Proof:

Since  $\check{\omega}$  is contntinuous, it can be represented by a continuous utility function  $u$ . By the definition of the term “utility representation”, finding an  $\check{\omega}$ -optimal bundle is equivalent to solving the problem  $\max_{x \in B(p, w)} u(x)$ . By the Weierstrass theorem, since the budget set is compact and  $u$  is continuous, the problem has a solution.

To emphasize that a utility representation is not necessary for the current analysis and that we could make do analysing the model using the concept of preferences, we will go through a direct proof of the previous claim that avoids using the notion of utility.

#### Direct Proof:

For any  $x \in B(p, w)$ , define the set  $Inferior(x) = \{y \in X | x \succ y\}$ . By the continuity of the preferences, every such set is open.

Assume there is no solution to the consumer problem of maximizing  $\gtrsim$  on  $B(p, w)$ . Then, every  $z \in B(p, w)$  is a member of some set  $Inferior(x)$ . Thus, the collection of open sets  $\{Inferior(x) \mid x \in B(p, w)\}$  covers  $B(p, w)$ . By the Heine-Borel theorem, this collection of open sets that covers a compact set has a finite sub-collection,  $\{Inferior(x^i)\}_{i \in I}$  that covers  $B(p, w)$ . Let  $x^j$  be the optimal bundle within the finite set  $\{x^1, \dots, x^n\}$ , then  $x^j$  is an optimal bundle in  $B(p, w)$ , a contradiction.

#### Claim:

- 1. If  $\zeta_{\infty}$  is convex, then the set of solutions to the consumer problem  $B(p, w)$  (or any other convex set) is convex.
- 2. If  $\zeta_{\infty}$  is strictly convex, then every consumer problem has at most one solution.

<!-- page 78 -->

#### Proof:

- 1. Assume that both  $x$  and  $y$  maximize  $\gtrsim$  given  $B(p, w)$ . By the convexity of the budget set  $B(p, w)$ , we have  $\alpha x + (1 - \alpha)y \in B(p, w)$ , and by the convexity of the preferences,  $\alpha x + (1 - \alpha)y \gtrsim x, y \gtrsim z$  for all  $z \in B(p, w)$ . Thus,  $\alpha x + (1 - \alpha)y$  is also a solution to the consumer problem.
- 2. Assume that both  $x$  and  $y$  (where  $x \neq y$ ) are solutions to the consumer problem  $B(p, w)$ . Then  $x \sim y$  (both  $x$  and  $y$  are solutions to the same maximization problem). The bundle  $\alpha x + (1 - \alpha)y$  is in  $B(p, w)$  (the budget set is convex). By the strict convexity of  $\succ$ ,  $\alpha x + (1 - \alpha)y \succ x$ , which is a contradiction of  $x$  being a maximal bundle in  $B(p, w)$ .

# The Consumer Problem with Differentiable Preferences

In the case that preferences are differentiable, the following condition characterizes the optimal solution: the “value per dollar” at a bundle of a commodity that is positively consumed is as large as the “value per dollar” of any other commodity.

#### Claim:

Assume that the consumer's preferences are differentiable and denote by  $v(x^*) = (v_1(x^*), \dots, v_K(x^*))$  the vector of "subjective value numbers" at  $x^*$  (see the definition of differentiable preferences in Lecture 4). If  $x^*$  is an optimal bundle in the consumer problem and  $k$  is a consumed commodity (i.e.,  $x_k^* > 0$ ), then it must be that  $v_k(x^*)/p_k \geq v_j(x^*)/p_j$  for all other  $j$ .

#### Proof:

Assume that  $x^*$  is a solution to the consumer problem  $B(p, w)$  and that  $x_k^* > 0$  and  $v_j(x^*)/p_j > v_k(x^*)/p_k$  for some  $j$  (see fig. 5.1). A “move” in the direction of reducing the consumption of the  $k$ ’th commodity by 1 and increasing the consumption of the  $j$ ’th commodity by  $p_k/p_j$  is an improvement direction since  $v_j(x^*)p_k/p_j - v_k(x^*) > 0$ . Since  $x_k^* > 0$ , we can find  $\varepsilon > 0$  small enough such that decreasing  $k$ ’s quantity by  $\varepsilon$  and increasing  $j$ ’s quantity by  $\varepsilon p_k/p_j$  is a feasible improvement. This brings the consumer to a strictly better bundle inside his budget set, contradicting the assumption that  $x^*$  is a solution to the consumer problem.

<!-- page 79 -->

![](pg_21__page_59_Figure_1.jpeg)

> **Image description:** Two graphs labelled (a) and (b) illustrating the consumer problem budget set $B(p,w)$ in two dimensions with axes $x_k$ (horizontal) and $x_j$ (vertical).

Graph (a) shows a linear budget line sloping downward with slope magnitude $p_k/p_j$. An indifference curve touches the budget line at an optimal consumption bundle point $x^* = (x_k^*, x_j^*)$.

Graph (b) demonstrates a perturbation around $x^*$. Decreasing consumption of good $k$ by $\epsilon$ to $x_k^* - \epsilon$ and increasing consumption of good $j$ by $\epsilon p_k/p_j$ to $x_j^* + \epsilon p_k / p_j$ stays on the budget constraint line, moving to a new point on a higher indifference curve, illustrating why a non-tangency or non-optimal choice provides a direction for feasible improvement.

Figure 5.1

(a)  $x^*$  is a solution to the consumer problem  $B(p, w)$ . (b)  $x^*$  is not a solution to the consumer problem  $B(p, w)$ .

#### Conclusion:

If  $x^*$  is a solution to the consumer problem  $B(p, w)$  and both  $x_k^* > 0$  and  $x_j^* > 0$ , then the ratio  $v_k(x^*)/v_j(x^*)$  must be equal to the price ratio  $p_k/p_j$ .

The above implies the “classic” necessary conditions on the consumer’s maximization when the preferences are represented by a differentiable utility function  $u$  with positive partial derivatives, and taking  $v_k(x^*)$  to be  $\partial u/\partial x_k(x^*)$ .

In order to establish sufficient conditions for maximization, we also require that the preferences be convex.

#### Claim:

If  $\mathscr{U}$  is strongly monotonic, convex, continuous, and differentiable, and if at  $x^*$ 

- $px^* = w$ ,
   $w_i(x^*)/p$ ,
- $v_k(x^*)/p_k \geq v_j(x^*)/p_j$  for any  $k$  such that  $x_k^* > 0$ , and for any  $j$ ,

then  $x^*$  is a solution to the consumer problem.

#### Proof:

Assume that  $x^*$  is not a solution. Then, there is a bundle  $y$  such that  $p \cdot y \leq w$  and  $y \succ x^*$ . By the comment at the end of the previous chapter,  $(y - x^*)$  is an improvement direction at  $x^*$ . Let  $\mu = v_k(x^*)/p_k$  for

<!-- page 80 -->

all  $k$  where  $x_k^* > 0$ . Now,

$$0 \geq p \cdot (y - x^*) = \sum p_k (y_k - x_k^*) \geq \sum \frac{v_k(x^*)}{\mu} (y_k - x_k^*)$$

since: (1)  $y$  is feasible and  $p \cdot x^* = w$ , (2) for a commodity  $k$  with  $x_k^* > 0$  we have  $p_k = v_k(x^*)/\mu$ , and (3) for a commodity  $k$  with  $x_k^* = 0$ ,  $(y_k - x_k^*) \geq 0$  and  $p_k \geq v_k(x^*)/\mu$ . Thus,  $0 \geq v(x^*) \cdot (y - x^*)$ , contradicting that  $(y - x^*)$  is an improvement direction at  $x^*$ .

# The Demand Function of a Rational Consumer

We have arrived at a critical stage on the way to developing a model in which we derive demand from preferences. Assume that the consumer's preferences are such that for any  $B(p, w)$ , the consumer's problem has a unique solution. Then, we identify the demand function  $x(p, w)$  with the maximizer of the consumer's preferences over  $B(p, w)$ . The domain of the demand function is  $\mathbb{R}_{++}^{K+1}$  and its range is  $\mathbb{R}_+^K$ .

#### Example:

Consider a consumer in a world with two commodities who has the following lexicographic preference relation, where the sum of the quantities of the goods is the first priority and the quantity of commodity 1 as the second, that is:

 $x \asymp y$  if  $[x_1 + x_2 > y_1 + y_2]$  or  $[x_1 + x_2 = y_1 + y_2$  and  $x_1 \geq y_1]$ . This preference relation is strictly convex but not continuous.

This preference relation is strictly convex but not continuous and still induces a (noncontinuous) demand function:

$$x((p_1, p_2), w) = \begin{cases} (0, w/p_2) & if \quad p_2 < p_1 \\ (w/p_1, 0) & if \quad p_2 \geq p_1 \end{cases}$$

We now turn to some properties of the demand function.

### Claim (Walras' Law):

If the preferences are monotonic, then any solution  $x$  to the consumer problem  $B(p, w)$  is located on its budget curve (that is,  $p \cdot x = w$ ).

#### Proof:

If not, then  $p \cdot x < w$  and for  $\varepsilon > 0$  small enough  $p \cdot (x_1 + \varepsilon, \dots, x_K + \varepsilon) < w$ . By monotonicity,  $(x_1 + \varepsilon, \dots, x_K + \varepsilon) \succ x$ , thus contradicting the assumption that  $x$  is optimal in  $B(p, w)$ .

<!-- page 81 -->

#### Claim:

If  $\zeta_{\infty}$  is a continuous preference relation, then the induced demand function is continuous in prices and in wealth.

#### Proof:

We can use the fact that the preferences have a continuous utility representation and apply a standard “maximum theorem”.<sup>1</sup>

And here is a direct proof that does not use the notion of a utility function:

Assume not. Then, there is a sequence of price and wealth vectors  $(p^n, w^n)$  converging to  $(p^*, w^*)$  such that  $x(p^*, w^*) = x^*$ , and  $x(p^n, w^n)$  does not converge to  $x^*$ . Thus, we can assume that  $(p^n, w^n)$  is a sequence converging to  $(p^*, w^*)$  such that for all  $n$  the distance  $d(x(p^n, w^n), x^*) > \varepsilon$  for some positive  $\varepsilon > 0$ .

All numbers  $p_k^n$  are greater than some positive number  $p_{\min}$  and all numbers  $w^n$  are less than some  $w^{\max}$ . Therefore, all vectors  $x(p^n, w^n)$  belong to some compact set (the hypercube of bundles with no quantity above  $w^{\max}/p_{\min}$ ), and thus, without loss of generality (choosing a subsequence if necessary), we can assume that  $x(p^n, w^n) \rightarrow y^*$  for some  $y^* \neq x^*$ .

Since  $p^n \cdot x(p^n, w^n) \leq w^n$  for all  $n$ , it must be that  $p^* \cdot y^* \leq w^*$ , that is,  $y^* \in B(p^*, w^*)$ . Since  $y^*$  is not a solution to the consumer problem  $B(p^*, w^*)$ , we have  $x^* \succ y^*$ . By the continuity of the preferences, there are neighborhoods  $B_{x^*}$  and  $B_{y^*}$  of  $x^*$  and  $y^*$  respectively in which the strict preference is preserved. For sufficiently large  $n$ ,  $x(p^n, w^n)$  is in  $B_{y^*}$ . Choose a bundle  $z^*$  in the neighborhood  $B_{x^*}$  so that  $p^* \cdot z^* < w^*$ . For sufficiently large  $n$ ,  $p^n \cdot z^* < w^n$ ; however,  $z^* \succ x(p^n, w^n)$ , a contradiction.

#### Comment:

The above is also a proof that if the preferences are continuous and  $x(p, w)$  is a demand *correspondence* ( $x(p, w)$  is the set of the consumer's maximizers over  $B(p, w)$ ) then the graph of the demand correspondence

<sup>1</sup> Let  $f : X \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$  be a continuous function. Let  $A$  be a subset of some Euclidean space. Let  $B$  be a correspondence from  $A$  into  $X$  that assigns to every  $a \in A$  nonempty compact subset of  $X$ , has a closed graph, and is lower hemicontinuous. That is, for every  $a \in A$  and every open set  $V \subseteq X$  such that  $B(a) \cap V \neq \emptyset$ , there exists a neighborhood  $U$  of  $a$  such that, for every  $a' \in U$ ,  $B(a') \cap V \neq \emptyset$ . Then the graph of the correspondence  $h$  from  $A$  into  $X$ , defined by  $h(a) = \{x \in B(a) \mid f(x) \geq f(y) \text{ for all } y \in B(a)\}$ , is closed.

<!-- page 82 -->

is closed, that is, if  $(p^n, w^n) \rightarrow (p^*, w^*)$  and a sequence  $x^n \in x(p^n, w^n)$  converges to  $x^*$  then  $x^* \in x(p^*, w^*)$ .

## Rationalizable Demand Functions

As in the general discussion of choice, we will now examine whether choice procedures are consistent with the rational man model. In what follows, various possible definitions of rationalization are considered.

One approach is to look for a preference relation (without imposing any restrictions that fit the context of the consumer) such that the chosen element from any budget set is the unique bundle maximizing the preference relation in that budget set. Thus, we say that the preferences  $\gtrsim$  *fully rationalize* the demand function  $x$  if for any  $(p, w)$  the bundle  $x(p, w)$  is the unique  $\gtrsim$ -maximal bundle within  $B(p, w)$ .

Alternatively, we can say that “being rationalizable” means that there are preferences such that the consumer’s behavior is consistent with maximizing those preferences, that is, for any  $(p, w)$  the bundle  $x(p, w)$  is a  $\zeta$ -maximal bundle (though not necessarily unique) within  $B(p, w)$ . This definition is “empty” since any demand function is consistent with maximizing the “total indifference” preference. This is why we usually say that the preferences  $\zeta$  *rationalize* the demand function  $x$  if they are *monotonic*, and for any  $(p, w)$  the bundle  $x(p, w)$  is a  $\zeta$ -maximal bundle within  $B(p, w)$ . Similar definitions would fit to other assumptions on the preferences. Of course, if behavior satisfies homogeneity of degree zero and Walras’ law, it is still not necessarily rationalizable according to the above definitions.

#### Example 1:

Consider the demand function of a consumer who spends all his wealth on the “more expensive” good:

$$x((p_1, p_2), w) = \begin{cases} (0, w/p_2) & \text{if } p_2 \geq p_1 \\ (w/p_1, 0) & \text{if } p_2 < p_1 \end{cases}$$

This demand function is not entirely “inconceivable”, and yet it is not rationalizable. To see this, assume that it is fully rationalizable or rationalizable by  $\succ$ . Consider the two budget sets  $B((1,2),1)$  and  $B((2,1),1)$ . Since  $x((1,2),1) = (0,1/2)$  and  $(1/2,0)$  is an internal bundle in  $B((1,2),1)$ , by either of the two definitions of rationalizability it must be that  $(0,1/2) \succ (1/2,0)$ . Similarly,  $x((2,1),1) = (1/2,0)$  and  $(0,1/2)$  is an internal bundle in  $B((2,1),1)$ . Therefore,  $(0,1/2) \prec (1/2,0)$ , a contradiction.

<!-- page 83 -->

#### Example 2:

A consumer chooses a bundle  $(z, z, \dots, z)$  where  $z$  satisfies  $z\Sigma p_k = w$ . This behavior is fully rationalized by any preferences according to which the consumer strictly prefers any bundle on the main diagonal over any bundle that is not (because, for example, he cares primarily about purchasing equal quantities from all sellers of the  $K$  goods), while on the main diagonal his preferences are based on “the more the better”. These preferences rationalize his behavior in the first sense but are not monotonic. This demand function is also fully rationalized by the monotonic preferences represented by the utility function  $u(x_1, \dots, x_K) = \min\{x_1, \dots, x_K\}$ .

#### Example 3:

Consider a consumer who spends  $\alpha_k$  of his wealth on commodity  $k$  (where  $\alpha_k > 0$  and  $\Sigma_{k=1}^K \alpha_k = 1$ ). This rule of behavior is not formulated as a maximization of some preference relation. Nonetheless, it can be fully rationalized by the preference relation represented by the Cobb-Douglas utility function  $u(x) = \prod_{k=1}^K x_k^{\alpha_k}$ , a differentiable function with strictly positive derivatives at all interior points. A solution  $x^*$  to the consumer problem  $B(p, w)$  must satisfy  $x_k^* > 0$  for all  $k$  (notice that  $u(x) = 0$  when  $x_k = 0$  for some  $k$ ). Given the differentiability of the preferences, a necessary condition for the optimality of  $x^*$  is that  $v_k(x^*)/p_k = v_l(x^*)/p_l$  for all  $k$  and  $l$  where  $v_k(x^*) = du/dx_k(x^*) = \alpha_k u(x^*)/x_k^*$  for all  $k$ . It follows that  $p_k x_k^*/p_l x_l^* = \alpha_k/\alpha_l$  for all  $k$  and  $l$  and thus  $x_k^* = \alpha_k w/p_k$  for all  $k$ .

#### Example 4:

Let  $K = 2$ . A consumer allocates his wealth between commodities 1 and 2 in the proportion  $p_2/p_1$  (the cheaper the good, the higher the share of wealth devoted to it). Thus,  $x_1p_1/x_2p_2 = p_2/p_1$  and  $x_i(p, w) = (p_j/(p_i + p_j))w/p_i$ .

To see that this demand function is fully rationalizable, note that  $x_i(p, w)/x_j(p, w) = p_j^2/p_i^2$  (for all  $i$  and  $j$ ) and therefore for every  $(p, w)$  we have  $p_1/p_2 = \sqrt{x_2(p, w)}/\sqrt{x_1(p, w)}$ . We look for a function for which the ratio of its partial derivatives is equal to  $\sqrt{x_2}/\sqrt{x_1}$ . The quasi-concave function  $\sqrt{x_1} + \sqrt{x_2}$  satisfies this condition. Thus, for any  $(p, w)$ , the bundle  $x(p, w)$  is the solution to the maximization of  $\sqrt{x_1} + \sqrt{x_2}$  in  $B(p, w)$ .

<!-- page 84 -->

![](pg_21__page_64_Figure_1.jpeg)

> **Image description:** Two panels, labeled (a) and (b), illustrating budget sets and chosen consumption bundles to test the Weak Axiom of Revealed Preference (WARP).

In both panels, two budget lines intersect in the positive quadrant of an x1-x2 graph:
- The steeper budget line represents the budget set B(p, w).
- The flatter budget line represents the budget set B(p', w').

In panel (a):
- The bundle x(p, w) is chosen on the steeper budget line B(p, w), located strictly above and outside the flatter budget set B(p', w').
- The bundle x(p', w') is chosen on the flatter budget line B(p', w'), located inside (affordable under) the steeper budget set B(p, w).
- Since x(p', w') was affordable when x(p, w) was chosen, but x(p, w) was not affordable when x(p', w') was chosen, this configuration does NOT violate the Weak Axiom of Revealed Preference.

In panel (b):
- The bundle x(p', w') is chosen on the flatter budget line B(p', w') above the intersection point, which lies inside (affordable under) the steeper budget set B(p, w).
- The bundle x(p, w) is chosen on the steeper budget line B(p, w) below the intersection point, which lies inside (affordable under) the flatter budget set B(p', w').
- Since each bundle was affordable when the other was chosen, this choice behavior violates the Weak Axiom of Revealed Preference.

Figure 5.2

(a) Satisfies the weak axiom. (b) Does not satisfy the weak axiom.

# The Weak and Strong Axioms of Revealed Preference

We now look for conditions that will guarantee that a demand function  $x(p, w)$  can be fully rationalized. A similar discussion would apply to the definition of rationalizability that requires the bundle  $x(p, w)$  to maximize a monotonic preference relation over  $B(p, w)$ . However, achieving such conditions is not necessary in order to determine whether a particular demand function is rationalizable. Guessing is often an excellent strategy.

In the general discussion of choice functions in Chapter 3, we saw that condition  $\alpha$  is necessary and sufficient for a choice function to be derived from some preference relation under certain assumptions about the domain of the choice function. The conditions there do not apply in the current setting since the domain of the demand function does not include finite sets and the union of two budget sets that do not include one another is not a budget set.

As in Lecture 3, we will use the concept of “revealed preference” induced from a demand function  $x(p, w)$ . We say that the bundle  $a$  is *revealed to be better* than the bundle  $b$  if there is  $(p, w)$  such that both  $a$  and  $b$  are in  $B(p, w)$  and  $a = x(p, w)$ . A demand function  $x(p, w)$  satisfies the *Weak Axiom of Revealed Preferences* if it is impossible that both  $a$  is revealed to be better than  $b$  and  $b$  is revealed to be better than  $a$ . In the context of the consumer model, the Weak Axiom can be written as: if  $x(p, w) \neq x(p', w')$  and  $p \cdot x(p', w') \leq w$ , then  $p' \cdot x(p, w) > w'$  (see fig. 5.2).

<!-- page 85 -->

The Weak Axiom states that the revealed preference binary relation is asymmetric. However, the relation is not necessarily complete since there can be two bundles  $x$  and  $y$  such that for any  $B(p, w)$  containing both of them,  $x(p, w)$  is neither  $x$  nor  $y$ . Apparently, the Weak Axiom is not a sufficient condition for extending the revealed preference into a complete and transitive relation (an example with three goods presented in Hicks (1956) is discussed in Mas-Colell et al. (1995)).

A necessary and sufficient condition for a demand function  $x$  (which satisfies Walras' law and homogeneity of degree zero) to be rationalizable is the Strong Axiom of Revealed Preferences which requires that the revealed preference derived from the demand function be acyclical. Note that the transitive closure of the revealed preference may still not be a complete relation. However, as mentioned above, the fact that the relation can be extended into a full-fledged preference relation is a well-known result in Set Theory. In any case, the Strong Axiom is somewhat cumbersome, and using it to determine whether a certain demand function is rationalizable may not be a straightforward task.

### Comment:

The more standard definition of rationalizability requires finding monotonic preferences  $\succ$  such that for any  $(p, w)$ ,  $x(p, w) \succ y$  for all  $y \in B(p, w)$ . We can then infer from the existence of a budget set  $B(p, w)$ , for which  $a = x(p, w)$  and  $b \in B(p, w)$ , only that  $a$  is weakly preferred to  $b$ . However, if  $p \cdot b < w$  as well, then we can further infer that  $a$  is strongly preferred to  $b$ .

# Decreasing Demand

A theoreoretical model can be evaluated by the reasonableness of its assumption but also of its implications. If we find that a model yields an absurd conclusion, then we might reconsider its assumptions and conclude that the assumptions are unreasonable or that we have assumed “too little”.

In the context of the consumer model, we might wonder whether the intuition that demand for a good falls when its price increases is a valid implication of the assumptions. We will now see that the classical assumptions of rational consumer behavior do not guarantee that demand is decreasing. The following is an example of a preference relation that induces demand which is nondecreasing in the price of one of the commodities.

<!-- page 86 -->

![](pg_21__page_66_Figure_1.jpeg)

> **Image description:** A 2D coordinate graph with x1 on the horizontal axis and x2 on the vertical axis, illustrating a nondecreasing demand example (Giffen good phenomenon) via budget lines and consumer choices. The vertical axis shows tick marks at 1/2 and 1. The horizontal axis shows tick marks at 1/4, 1, 3/2, and 2. Several budget lines connect points on the vertical axis to the horizontal axis: one line connects (0, 1) to (1, 0); another line connects (0, 1/2) to (1/4, 0); others connect (0, 1/2) to (1, 0), (3/2, 0), and (2, 0). Four labeled points represent consumer choices or consumption bundles: point 'a' is located at (0, 1/2) on the vertical axis; point 'b' is located at (1, 0) on the horizontal axis; point 'c' is in the interior near x1 = 0.8, x2 = 0.2; and point 'd' is located at (2, 0) on the horizontal axis.

Figure 5.3

An example in which demand increases with price ( $p_2 = 2$  and  $w = 1$ ). When  $2 < p_1$  the demand is at  $a$ . When  $1 < p_1 < 2$  the demand is to the left of  $b$ . When  $1/2 < p_1 < 1$  the demand is at a point like  $c$ . When  $p_1 < 1/2$  the demand is to the right of  $d$ .

Consider the preferences represented by the following utility function:

$$u(x_1, x_2) = \begin{cases} x_1 + x_2 & if x_1 + x_2 < 1 \\ x_1 + 4x_2 & if x_1 + x_2 \geq 1 \end{cases}$$

These preferences might reflect reasoning of the following type: “In the bundle  $x$  there are  $x_1 + x_2$  units of vitamin A and  $x_1 + 4x_2$  units of vitamin B. My first priority is to get enough vitamin A. However, once I satisfy my need for 1 unit of vitamin A, I will move on to my second priority, which is to consume as much vitamin B as possible” (See fig. 5.3.).

Consider  $x((p_1, 2), 1)$ . Changing  $p_1$  is like rotating the budget lines around the pivot bundle  $(0, 1/2)$ . At a high price of  $p_1$  (as long as  $p_1 > 2$ ), the consumer demands  $(0, 1/2)$ . If the price is reduced to within the range  $2 > p_1 \geq 1$ , the consumer chooses the bundle  $(1/p_1, 0)$ . So far, the demand for the first commodity indeed increases when its price falls. However, in the range  $1 > p_1 > 1/2$  we encounter an anomaly: the consumer buys as much as possible from the second good subject to the “constraint” that the sum of the goods is at least 1, that is,  $x((p_1, 2), 1) = (1/(2 - p_1), (1 - p_1)/(2 - p_1))$ .

The above preferences are monotonic, convex but not continuous. Nonetheless we can construct similar continuous preferences that induce a demand function which is increasing in  $p_1$  in a similar domain. For  $\delta > 0$ , let  $\alpha_\delta(t)$  be a continuous and increasing function on  $\Re_+$  so that  $\alpha_\delta(t) = 0$  for all  $t \leq 1 - \delta$  and  $\alpha_\delta(t) = 1$  for all  $t \geq 1$ . The utility function

$$u_\delta(x) = \alpha_\delta(x_1 + x_2)(x_1 + 4x_2) + (1 - \alpha_\delta(x_1 + x_2))(x_1 + x_2)$$

<!-- page 87 -->

is continuous and monotonic. For  $\delta$  close to 0, the function  $u_\delta = u$  except in a narrow area below the set of bundles for which  $x_1 + x_2 = 1$ . When  $p_1 = 2/3$ , the demand for the first commodity is 3/4, whereas when  $p_1 = 1$ , the demand is at least 1 –  $2\delta$ . Thus, for a small enough  $\delta$  the increase in  $p_1$  might lead to an increase in demand.

And another example. Consinsider the world with discrete good (which can be consumed only in non-negative integers). A consumer has strictly increasing preferences satisfying  $(1, 1) \succ (2, 0) \succ (1, 0) \succ (0, 1)$ . Assume  $p_2 = 2.5$  and  $w = 4$ . Then, the demand for good 1 at  $p_1 = 2$  is 2 and at  $p_1 = 1$  is only 1. Is this example better than the previous one?

# "The Law of Demand"

We are interested in comparing demand in different environments. We have just seen that the classic assumptions about consumer behavior do not lead to an unambiguous relationship between a consumer's demand when facing the budget sets  $B(p, w)$  and  $B(p + (0, \dots, \varepsilon, \dots, 0), w)$ .

In contrast, a clear conclusion can be drawn when we compare the consumer's demand when facing the budget set  $B(p, w)$  to his demand when facing  $B(p', x(p, w) \cdot p')$ . In this comparison, we imagine the price vector changing from  $p$  to an arbitrary  $p'$  and wealth changing in such a way that the consumer has exactly the resources allowing him to consume the same bundle he consumed at  $(p, w)$  (see fig. 5.4). The following claim implies that the compensated demand function  $y(p') = x(p', p' \cdot x(p, w))$  satisfies the law of demand, that is,  $y_k$  is decreasing in  $p_k$ . Notice that the claim holds for any demand function and not necessarily to one which induces from a maximization of some preference relation.

<!-- page 88 -->

![](pg_21__page_68_Figure_1.jpeg)

> **Image description:** A graph on a two-dimensional plane with horizontal axis x_1 and vertical axis x_2, illustrating budget sets and consumer demand choices. Two budget lines intersect in the positive quadrant:

1. A steeper budget line corresponding to the budget set B(p, w) extends from a high intercept on the x_2 axis to a low intercept on the x_1 axis.
2. A flatter budget line corresponding to the budget set B(p', w') extends from a lower intercept on the x_2 axis to a higher intercept on the x_1 axis.

The intersection point of the two budget lines is marked with a black dot labeled x(p, w).
Another point on the flatter budget line B(p', w'), located to the right (downward along the flatter line) of the intersection, is marked with a black dot labeled x(p', w').

Figure 5.4

A compensated price change from  $(p, w)$  to  $(p', w')$ .

#### Claim:

Let  $x$  be a demand function satisfying Walras' law and WA and  $B(p, w)$  some budget set. Let  $w' = p' \cdot x(p, w)$ , then either  $x(p', w') = x(p, w)$  or  $[p' - p] \cdot [x(p', w') - x(p, w)] < 0$ .

#### Proof:

Assume that  $x(p', w') \neq x(p, w)$ . By Walras' law and the assumption that  $w' = p' \cdot x(p, w)$ :

$$\begin{aligned} & [p' - p] \cdot [x(p', w') - x(p, w)] \\ &= p' \cdot x(p', w') - p' \cdot x(p, w) - p \cdot x(p', w') + p \cdot x(p, w) \\ &= w' - w' - p \cdot x(p', w') + w = w - p \cdot x(p', w') \end{aligned}$$

By WA, the right-hand side of the equation is less than 0.

