### Problem 6. (Moderately difficult)

It is a common practice in economics to view aggregate demand as being derived from the behavior of a “representative consumer”. Construct an example of two consumer preference relations that induce average behavior which is not consistent with maximization by a “representative consumer”. That is, describe two rational “consumers”, 1 and 2, who choose the bundles  $x^1$  and  $x^2$  from the budget set  $A$  and the bundles  $y^1$  and  $y^2$  from the budget set  $B$  so that the choice of the bundle  $(x^1 + x^2)/2$  from  $A$  and of the bundle  $(y^1 + y^2)/2$  from  $B$  are inconsistent with the model of the rational consumer.

#### Problem 7. (Standard)

A commodity  $k$  is *Giffen* if the demand for commodity  $k$  is increasing in  $p_k$ . A commodity  $k$  is *inferior* if the demand for the commodity decreases with wealth. Show that if there is a vector  $(p, w)$  such that the demand for commodity  $k$  increases with its price, then there is a vector  $(p', w')$  such that demand for commodity  $k$  falls when the  $w$  increases (being Giffen implies that the commodity is inferior).

