- (i) if one of the vectors dominates the other he evaluates it as superior.
- (ii) if  $x_1 > y_1$  and  $y_2 > x_2$ , he carries out a “cancellation” operation and then makes the evaluation by comparing  $(x_1 - y_1, 0)$  to  $(0, y_2 - x_2)$ , which is accomplished by comparing  $v_1(x_1 - y_1)$  with  $v_2(y_2 - x_2)$  (similarly, if  $x_1 < y_1$  and  $x_2 > y_2$ , he bases his preference on the comparison of  $v_2(x_2 - y_2)$  to  $v_1(y_1 - x_1)$ ).
- Verify that if  $v_i^*(t) = t$  (for both  $i$ ), then the procedure  $P(v_1^*, v_2^*)$  induces a preference relation on  $\Re_+^2$ .Explain why  $P(v_1, v_2)$  does not necessarily lead to a transitive preference relation on  $\Re_+^2$ .
