#### Problem A23. (NYU 2025)

In this question a choice problem is not a set but rather a *multi-set*, where elements can appear more than once. Let  $X$  be a finite grand set of alternatives. Formally, a multi-set  $A$  is specified by a function  $A(x)$  which describes the number of times  $x$  appears in  $A$ . For example, if  $X = \{a, b, c\}$  the multiset  $A = [a, a, b]$  is specified by  $A(a) = 2$ ,  $A(b) = 1$  and  $A(c) = 0$ . A choice function on the set  $X$  assigns to every choice problem  $A$  one of the elements for which  $A(x) > 0$ .

The following are properties of a choice function  $C$ :

Property I: If  $C(A) = a$  and  $B$  is obtained from A by adding one appearance of  $a$  then  $C(B) = a$ .

Property II. If  $A(a) = B(a) =k$  and  $A(b) = B(b) = l$  and  $C(A) = a$ , then  $C(B) \neq b$ .

