# Problem D5. (NYU 2012. Based on Rubinstein (1980).)

An individual is comparing pairs of alternatives within a finite set  $X$  ( $|X| \geq 3$ ). His comparison yields unambiguous results, such that either  $x$  is evaluated to be better than  $y$  (denoted  $x \rightarrow y$ ) or  $y$  is evaluated to be better than  $x$  ( $y \rightarrow x$ ). A ranking method assigns to each such relation  $\rightarrow$  (namely, complete, irreflexive and antisymmetric relation, but not necessarily transitive) a preference relation  $\succ$  ( $\rightarrow$ ) over  $X$ . Consider the following axioms with respect to ranking methods:

- (i) *Neutrality*: “the names of the alternatives are immaterial”. (Formally, let  $\sigma$  be a permutation of  $X$  and let  $\sigma(\rightarrow)$  be the relation defined by  $\sigma(x)\sigma(\rightarrow)\sigma(y)$  iff  $x \rightarrow y$ . Then,  $x \asymp (\rightarrow)y$  iff  $\sigma(x) \asymp (\sigma(\rightarrow))\sigma(y)$ .)

  (ii) *Monotonicity*: if  $x \asymp (\rightarrow)y$ , then  $x \asymp (\rightarrow')y$  where  $\rightarrow'$  differs from  $\rightarrow$ .
- (ii) *Monotonicity*: if  $x \asymp (\rightarrow)y$ , then  $x \succ (\rightarrow')y$  where  $\rightarrow'$ , differs from  $\rightarrow$  only in the existence of one alternative  $z$  such that  $z \rightarrow x$  and  $x \rightarrow' z$ .

<!-- page 182 -->

- (iii) *Independence:* The ranking between any two alternatives depends only on the results of comparisons that involve at least one of the two alternatives.

